  "analysis": [
    {
      "id": "1294402688213057536",
      "headline": "Stilly Finds Quarterback Cover as JMid and Thommy Reshape Their Receiving Corps",
      "analysis": "Stilly's Week 10 starter Darnold scored 4.92 while Daniels was on the bench at zero. Brown started for 2.30 and Chase was benched at zero. Goff (12.10), St. Brown (5.20) and Addison (9.00) all started Week 11 for 26.30 combined. JMid exchanged St. Brown for Chase, a like-position move; his Week 10 lineup used four WRs, and Week 11 Chase started while Odunze joined him at WR and both FLEX slots went to RBs. Thommy surrendered his Week 10 QB starter Goff (24.80) and WR starter Addison (5.00), but had Mahomes on the bench at zero that week; Mahomes started Week 11 (13.34), Brown started (8.40), and Higgins, Nacua and Williams completed the receiving group. The move gave Stilly QB cover, JMid a different lead receiver and Thommy a receiver while relying on Mahomes. Other Week 11 changes and the Diggs transaction cannot be attributed to the three-team deal.",
      "verdict": "Stilly's three-player return addressed an observable quarterback need and added receiver options. JMid pursued a receiver-for-receiver upgrade rather than positional rebalancing. Thommy consolidated two assets into one receiver, relying on an existing quarterback. Both the benefit of that consolidation and keeper value require independent review."
    },
    {
      "id": "1294424036499025920",
      "headline": "Stover Gets Immediate Lineup Help While Stilly Trades for an Unused Receiver",
      "analysis": "Week 10 Diggs started for Stilly (13.10); Thomas started for Stover (0). Week 11 Diggs started for Stover (15.00), while Thomas was on Stilly's bench at zero. The Diggs deal was completed 224426 ms before the three-team trade. Stilly's later acquisition of St. Brown and Addison materially changed receiver depth; evaluate the two deals independently and in sequence. The archived Diggs total of 7.10 across two starts conflicts with the Week 11 starter snapshot of 15.00 and requires reconciliation before grading.",
      "verdict": "Stover received immediate lineup production from a previously unproductive slot. Stilly's acquisition did not start in the observed week, but his broader receiving changes complicate the strategic assessment."
    },
    {
      "id": "1294724519813988352",
      "headline": "Stew Seeks Tight End Help as Kuhl Upgrades His Backfield Options",
      "analysis": "Week 10 Stew started Ertz (7.40), Henry (9.90), Gibbs (37.70) and Henderson (29.50); Kraft was benched at zero. Kuhl started Hunter Henry at TE (1.40), Warren at RB (10.10), Kyren Williams and Kamara, with Ferguson on the bench at zero. Week 11 Stew started Ferguson (9.60) and Warren (7.70), with Gibbs/Henderson in FLEX; Kuhl started Ertz (6.20) and Derrick Henry (20.20). Both acquisitions started on both sides; the deal exchanged players at the same two positions rather than changing positional counts. Kuhl's earlier acquisition of Collins and Stew's earlier acquisition of Henry are separate, with Henry's September acquisition verified in archive but ownership continuity pending.",
      "verdict": "Both managers reworked existing starting positions. Kuhl extracted the stronger immediate two-player scoring return (26.40 versus 17.30), while Stew added a tight end and back to an already productive backfield. Long-run lineup improvement and the Henry trade chain require reconciliation."
